A||N||9||FieroSTETZ||water cooling a v-6 bug...||||1||
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||09-29-2000||09:01 PM||||my brother and I have just finished buying a rebuilt engine (performance rebuild, high compression, 1600 cc's, 120 hp) for the 2nd bug of the family of cars, and it cost a whopping 1190 dollars! we got it from a local bug performance place along with monza exhaust, a 2x2bbl intake manifold, and 2 mikuni carbs. this bug ought to go fairly well. now onto the questions- i heard a guy talking about putting a 2.8 from a cavalier into a beetle and wondered, could you set up a beetle for water cooling, and could a 2.8 be used? with the torque and hp of a relatively cheap engine like that, that car would probably kick a$s. anyone with info, i would be greatly appreciated. BYW- soon i will have new video footage for you all of something new i finally finished on my gt. hint: it hisses in the high rev's- watch for more, same fiero time, same fiero channel. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1012904&a=7460599&p=25516673"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Greg Piet||09-29-2000||11:39 PM||||I guesss I am old school, but a bug cannot haev water cooling...   That is why I do not consider the "new" beetles bugs...<P>But then again I have a moral dilemma with a water cooled Porsche.<P>I know in some of the VW mags I saw 220+ hp air cooled bug engines which would be a big improvement from my 36 hp vw engine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||KitKar||09-30-2000||01:59 AM||||I don't know about in a bug, but in a Sterling Kit car you can put in a water cooled engine in and mount 2 small radiators in the back.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||09-30-2000||08:02 AM||||We have a '70 CarmanGhia convertible sitting here in the garage under restoration. It needs a new engine and tranny, and I jokingly pointed at the 2.8 I just bought. It makes perfect sense. It will fit fine back there, could use a Fiero tranny with custom axles and viola, a Ghia putting out 170+lbft of toque. All thats needed is to figure out the water cooling, wiring, electronics, etc. Probably won't happen, looking into another(easier)option right now.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rockcrawl||09-30-2000||08:20 PM||||In my opinion, air cooled Volkswagens are only good for one thing, building dune buggies! Here's one I built about five years ago.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/buggy.jpg"> <P>I also found this website that might interest some of you beetle-lovers. The maniac put a 500 ci Caddi engine in the back of a beetle, very similar to the way I did a 350 V8 Fiero/Countach. If that thing can stay cool, a 2.8 should be no problem. <A HREF="http://www.wsu.edu/~426hemi/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.wsu.edu/~426hemi/</A> <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||09-30-2000||08:58 PM||||I just checked out that guy's Caddywagon- Damn that is funny! That has to be the coolest use of an old Bug that I have seen in a long time.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cheever2||09-30-2000||09:08 PM||||That was insane.<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000007||fierospeeder||10-01-2000||02:43 AM||||i own a 74 beetle, and totally love them.<P>Ive seen v8s put on beetles in the front.<P>There are adapters to put a corvair engine and also a ford 2.3l engine to fit on the rear of the car on the trans.<P>i know the 2.3l is watercooled, i have one on my tbird with a turbo.  <P>I havn't seen anything for a chevy engine.  plus you might need to cut the hood and make it look like one of those baja cars.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cheever2||10-01-2000||02:52 AM||||When I was into Bugs, I wanted to someday take the body and turn it around facing backwards.  Would've put pop-up headlights in the rear fenders (now the front), and would have to put all the electricals and steering brackets coming up from the floor, then fabricate here & there to get the body to match.  But it would be interesting.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||rockcrawl||10-01-2000||09:31 AM||||For anyone who is serious about VW transplants, here are a few links to adapter manufacturers, the second one has what you need to bolt the 2.8 to the VW transaxle:<P>VW transaxle / VW Golf-Rabbit-Jetta engines, Nissan 1400, Toyota 2.2, 2.4 diesel    <A HREF="http://www.giloeng.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.giloeng.com/</A> <P>VW Transaxle / most any engine <A HREF="http://www.kennedyeng.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.kennedyeng.com/</A> <P><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
